# Target make.conf — used via emerge --config-root for everything merged into the image.
# Rendered by stage 20 — placeholders below are substituted from build.conf / runtime.

CHOST="x86_64-pc-linux-gnu"
COMMON_FLAGS="-march=x86-64 -O2 -pipe"   # generic baseline: no AVX2 floor (see plan/03)
CFLAGS="${COMMON_FLAGS}"
CXXFLAGS="${COMMON_FLAGS}"
MAKEOPTS="-j@JOBS@"

# Global USE. The desktop half of this is Plasma-shaped (plan/09); everything above the
# first line break is desktop-agnostic and unchanged from the GNOME image.
#
#   -handbook   KDE ships a DocBook handbook per app. Nothing in the image can render one
#               (no khelpcenter, no yelp), and they install under /usr/share/doc/HTML, which
#               stage 50 deletes anyway. Off at the source instead.
#   -telemetry  drops kde-frameworks/kuserfeedback and the KDE telemetry surface with it.
#   -webengine  the structural guard against dev-qt/qtwebengine — a Chromium build. Nothing
#               native renders HTML here (plan/03); backed by a hard mask in package.mask.
#   -designer   Qt Designer plugins, for an image with no IDE.
#   -gnome -gnome-keyring -gnome-online-accounts
#               global levers that stop packages offering GNOME integration from pulling it
#               in. -gnome is load-bearing for x11-misc/xdg-utils specifically (see the long
#               note in package.use/image).
#
#   -introspection
#               was "introspection" (positive), because gjs and gnome-shell load GObject
#               typelibs at runtime. Nothing in the image does that any more, and most of the
#               packages that build them default the flag ON in their own IUSE — so dropping
#               the token was not enough; it has to be negated. dev-libs/glib keeps it as a
#               per-package line below so the binhost's glib binpkg still matches.
#               ROLLBACK: if some surviving package turns out to dlopen a typelib, put
#               "introspection" back here AND revert the girepository-1.0 deletion in stage 50
#               — that pair moves together, in both directions.
#   -vala       .vapi files are build-time inputs for compiling Vala, and nothing in the image
#               compiles anything — stage 50 deletes usr/share/vala outright, so the flag only
#               ever built files the prune then threw away. It is also what makes
#               -introspection viable at all: several packages (gnome-base/librsvg,
#               app-crypt/libsecret) carry REQUIRED_USE="vala? ( introspection )", so with vala
#               left on, -introspection is unsatisfiable and the depgraph stops.
USE="wayland pipewire screencast bluetooth vulkan vaapi \
     -doc -gtk-doc -examples -test \
     -handbook -telemetry -webengine -designer \
     -gnome -gnome-keyring -gnome-online-accounts -introspection -vala"

VIDEO_CARDS="amdgpu radeonsi intel virgl nvidia"
# nouveau dropped: nvidia-drivers ships /etc/modprobe.d/nvidia.conf blacklisting nouveau
# unconditionally (plan/03), so it was never a live fallback, only mesa/xorg-driver bytes
# for a module that can't load. See plan/03 and plan/10 for the tradeoff this removes.
INPUT_DEVICES="libinput"
L10N="@L10N@"

ACCEPT_LICENSE="-* @FREE"
# proprietary exceptions live in package.license/

FEATURES="nodoc noinfo noman buildpkg"
PKGDIR="/cache/binpkgs"
DISTDIR="/cache/distfiles"

# NB: no getbinpkg and no PORTAGE_BINHOST here, deliberately. Everything that ships in the
# image is compiled by this pipeline, from the pinned snapshot, against the USE and CFLAGS
# above — or reused from a binpkg an earlier run of this pipeline compiled. That reuse is what
# buildpkg + PKGDIR are for: /cache is a named volume, so a rebuild is not a recompile.
#
# The binhost's copies are built against the DEFAULT profile's USE, and portage merges a
# remote binpkg whenever its recorded USE happens to satisfy the graph. That makes "what
# ships" a function of what the binhost published that day rather than of this repo, and the
# difference is not always visible in the USE string: package.use/image documents a
# from-scratch build where x11-misc/xdg-utils resolved to a binhost copy built with gnome+dbus
# and the image inherited ~37 perl packages from that binpkg's baked RDEPEND, which a source
# build of the same ebuild does not pull.
#
# The BUILDER is the opposite case and is configured the opposite way: its own tools (qemu,
# dracut, rsvg-convert, ...) are stock packages with no image-specific requirements, so it
# installs them as binaries from BINHOST_URI (builder/Dockerfile). BINHOST_URI in build.conf
# is the builder's setting and only the builder's; nothing it fetches reaches the image.

# NB: there is deliberately no INSTALL_MASK here. Portage applies INSTALL_MASK to every
# merge in an emerge invocation, and this pipeline's emerge populates TWO roots: runtime deps
# into $TARGET and build deps into the builder's own "/". A mask set here therefore also
# stripped headers, *.pc, cmake and *.gir files from the build dependencies on "/", so any
# later source build that needed them failed to configure — e.g. gnome-system-monitor dying on
# "Package 'vulkan', required by 'gtkmm-4.0', not found", and the same root cause behind the
# tecla lookup. The dev-file stripping now happens in stage 50 against $TARGET only, where it
# cannot affect the builder (plan/06).
